{
  "passage": "Poor households in villages often find it hard to get loans from banks. Banks ask for collateral, such as land or a building, that can be sold if the loan is not repaid, and many poor people have nothing to offer. They are forced to borrow from moneylenders, who may charge interest as high as five per cent a month. Self Help Groups, or SHGs, were formed to solve this problem. A typical SHG has 15 to 20 members, usually women from the same village. Each member saves a small amount every month, perhaps fifty or a hundred rupees. Over time, the savings become a useful fund. Members can take small loans from the group to meet their needs, and the group decides the purpose, the amount and the repayment schedule. The interest charged is far lower than that of a moneylender. After a year or two of regular saving and repayment, the group becomes eligible for a loan from a bank. The bank lends to the group as a whole, without collateral, because the members are responsible for repaying together. With such loans, women have started small businesses such as tailoring, poultry and making pickles. SHGs also give women a chance to meet regularly and discuss issues such as health, education and domestic violence. The Grameen Bank of Bangladesh, started by Muhammad Yunus, is a well-known example of this idea.",
